domain InitialAreaOwnReference:
  description "Bounded dictionary ownership guard repair"
problem P1:
  "Canonical initial area references must resolve to declared own entries."
evidence E1:
  |
    The strengthened structured-battlefield test changes a mapped character area to
    toString without declaring that area. Before-repair TAP fails Missing expected
    rejection. Canonical/initial layout guards check areas[id] truthiness.
evidence E2:
  |
    Plain JavaScript objects inherit toString and constructor. A property lookup can
    return an inherited function even when the record has no own area entry.
    The legacy creation helper also tests areas[id] to detect duplicates/collisions.
    Existing StableBattlefieldIdSchema allows these letter-prefixed stable IDs.
evidence E3:
  |
    This is a pure local regression, not model, transport or production observation.
    Accepted stable ID and canonical reference rules require declared own areas;
    banning legal IDs or choosing a replacement would alter the contract.
    Existing saved malformed snapshots and historical introduction remain unknown.
decision D1 based_on P1, E1, E2, E3:
  |
    The producing condition is treating inherited object properties as canonical
    record entries. The toString reference is the trigger; ordinary invented-ID
    cases missed this dictionary ownership distinction. Model behavior is not the
    cause on this pure constructor path.
decision D2 based_on D1, E3:
  |
    Use own-key checks for canonical/initial area and entity references and legacy
    duplicate/collision detection, validating declared legacy IDs before writing.
    Preserve legal constructor IDs when actually declared. Reverify the full shared
    and affected current HTTP cohort and refresh the changed source/test seals.
